  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Breezy winds 15 to 25 MPH on Wednesday could cause choppy\n   lakes. A few thunderstorms are possible.\n\n-  Warmest temperatures of the summer this weekend through early\n   next week. \n\nShower and thunderstorm activity will be limited today as high \npressure begins to build over the region. While most areas will \nremain dry, there is still a slight chance for a few isolated \nstorms along the Continental Divide into southwest Montana. \nTemperatures will trend several degrees warmer than Monday.\n\nA fast-moving weather system will bring breezy westerly winds \nto the region on Wednesday and Thursday. Expect sustained winds \nbetween 15 and 25 mph, which will likely create choppy and \nhazardous conditions on area lakes for small crafts. Additionally,\nsouthwest Montana will see a renewed chance for afternoon \nthunderstorms on Wednesday.\n\nA strong high-pressure system centered over the Four Corners will \nbegin expanding into the Northern Rockies by Friday, ushering in \na drier and significantly warmer weather pattern. High \ntemperatures will climb into the 80s and 90s across western \nMontana, while the Hells Canyon and Salmon River Valley regions of\nnorth-central Idaho could see temperatures peak near 102 degrees.\nMoisture will be limited, but a few isolated, terrain-driven \nshowers or thunderstorms may develop over southwest Montana.\n\nExcessive heat will peak over the weekend and persist through \nMonday as hot air surges northward from the Great Basin. There is\na high probability (70 to 80 percent or greater) that high \ntemperatures will reach 100 to 105 degrees in the lower valleys of\nnorth- central Idaho and Lemhi County. Given these extreme \ntemperatures, it is critical to practice heat safety: stay \nhydrated, take frequent cooling breaks if working outdoors during \nthe afternoon, and never leave children or pets unattended in \nvehicles.\n\nLooking further ahead, forecast models indicate the potential for a\nsignificant surge of subtropical moisture to enter the region between \nJuly 13th and 15th. This weather pattern shift would increase the\nrisk for widespread, rain-producing thunderstorms across western \nMontana and north central Idaho.",
